<p>I accidentally logged into <a href="https://baty.blog/">baty.blog</a>'s admin and said, &quot;SQUIRREL!&quot;, and spent time taking another look at the site. Who knows, I might start using it again.</p>
<!-- more -->
<p>Baty.blog runs on <a href="https://pureblog.org/">Pure Blog</a>, by <a href="https://kevquirk.com/">Kev Quirk</a>. When Kev first announced Pure Blog, I (of course) tried it immediately. We got along well, right off the bat. Pure Blog is written in PHP, so it's simple to install nearly anywhere. It uses plain-text Markdown files instead of a database. It did all the basics that a blogging CMS should do. It aimed to be simple-but-not-too-simple. At the time, I found it to be a little too simple, so I abandoned it, meaning to check on it again, later.</p>
<p>It's later.</p>
<p>A <a href="https://github.com/kevquirk/pureblog/releases">lot has changed</a> since I last used v1.2.0. Kev's been busy! Anyway, things looked stable and it had gotten important fixes and updates, so I upgraded both Pure Blog and my <a href="https://purecomments.org/">Pure Comments</a> instance. I wired up comment emails, since Pure Comments can now use regular ol' SMTP (it used to be only AWS SES, gross).</p>
<p>I have cleaned up the layout I was using so it at least looks presentable. It seems I'm once again posting to baty.blog. For now. Don't forget, there's an <a href="https://baty.blog/feed">RSS feed</a>.</p>
